{"task": "Prove exactly this local conditional obligation using the supplied NL route. Return only the tactic body through submit_transition. All displayed parameter and input proof binders are already in context.", "packet": {"version": 2, "contract_sha256": "3575d5d75c1eb2aaf8b92e3182b02e09afba2fd0f4d1ab9e569e2cc16c872495", "header": "import Mathlib

open scoped ENNReal NNReal BigOperators

abbrev YuiEucl (n : ℕ) := EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin n)
def YuiI : Set ℝ := Set.Icc 0 1
def YuiSnoc {n : ℕ} (x : YuiEucl n) (t : ℝ) : YuiEucl (n + 1) :=
  WithLp.toLp 2 (Fin.snoc (fun i => x i) t)
def YuiHead {n : ℕ} (z : YuiEucl (n + 1)) : YuiEucl n :=
  WithLp.toLp 2 (fun i : Fin n => z i.castSucc)
def YuiLast {n : ℕ} (z : YuiEucl (n + 1)) : ℝ := z (Fin.last n)
def YuiCylinder {n : ℕ} (E : Set (YuiEucl n)) : Set (YuiEucl (n + 1)) :=
  {z | YuiHead z ∈ E ∧ YuiLast z ∈ YuiI}
noncomputable def YuiCost {X : Type*} [PseudoEMetricSpace X] {ι : Type*}
    (s : ℝ) (U : ι → Set X) : ℝ≥0∞ :=
  ∑' i, ⨆ (_ : (U i).Nonempty), (Metric.ediam (U i)) ^ s
def YuiCover {X : Type*} [PseudoEMetricSpace X] (δ : ℝ) (A : Set X)
    (U : ℕ → Set X) : Prop :=
  A ⊆ ⋃ i, U i ∧ ∀ i, Metric.ediam (U i) ≤ ENNReal.ofReal δ
noncomputable def YuiContent {X : Type*} [PseudoEMetricSpace X]
    (s δ : ℝ) (A : Set X) : ℝ≥0∞ :=
  ⨅ (U : ℕ → Set X) (_ : YuiCover δ A U), YuiCost s U
noncomputable def YuiMeasure {X : Type*} [PseudoEMetricSpace X]
    (s : ℝ) (A : Set X) : ℝ≥0∞ :=
  ⨆ (δ : ℝ) (_ : 0 < δ), YuiContent s δ A
", "parameters": [{"name": "n", "type": "ℕ"}, {"name": "E", "type": "Set (YuiEucl n)"}], "representation": "Use EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin n)=PiLp 2 (constant ℝ), indexed by Fin n, with its native L2 metric and Borel structure; n is a natural ambient dimension including 0. YuiSnoc appends a real coordinate and YuiHead/YuiLast recover it; YuiCylinder E is defined by these coordinates directly in EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin (n+1)). T003 checks the coordinate inverse and Euclidean distance identities used by all cylinder arguments, so there is no max-product metric or unproved metric replacement. The interval remains Set.Icc (0:ℝ) 1. Metric.ediam, YuiCost, YuiContent and YuiMeasure take values in ℝ≥0∞; ^ with a real exponent is ENNReal.rpow. The supremum over a nonempty witness equals ediam^s and over an empty witness equals zero, giving the prescribed empty-cover convention even at exponent zero. All uses of Hausdorff content or normalization are guarded by s>0 and δ>0. Cover sequences enumerate countable covers with empty padding; the positive-diameter construction uses a subset of ℕ to avoid duplicating finite nonempty families. dimH is native, defined as the supremum of nonnegative exponents with infinite native unnormalized Hausdorff measure; its nonnegative extended codomain gives dimH empty=0. Positive real exponents are embedded by ENNReal.ofReal only with the relevant positivity; D remains dimH E in ℝ≥0∞ and its toReal value is used only downstream of T005's finiteness. Nat casts of n and constants are finite ENNReal values. Lebesgue measure/integrals in the slicing and interval workers are volume on ℝ and the nonnegative lintegral. All five M facts are supplied by direct pinned Mathlib use, not additional premises. Background notes remain optional node-local references. N outputs are unchanged; auxiliary T001 exposes the uniform metric-space normalization calculation already used in S001 so both Euclidean and real-interval instances can share it.", "inputs": [{"id": "T001", "binder": "h_T001", "type": "∀ (X : Type) [MetricSpace X] [MeasurableSpace X] [BorelSpace X] (A : Set X) (a : ℝ), 0 < a → YuiMeasure a A = MeasureTheory.Measure.hausdorffMeasure a A"}], "target": "(∀ (k : ℕ) (A : Set (YuiEucl k)) (a : ℝ), 0 < a → YuiMeasure a A = MeasureTheory.Measure.hausdorffMeasure a A ∧ (dimH A < ENNReal.ofReal a → YuiMeasure a A = 0) ∧ (ENNReal.ofReal a < dimH A → YuiMeasure a A = ⊤) ∧ (YuiMeasure a A = 0 → dimH A ≤ ENNReal.ofReal a) ∧ (YuiMeasure a A = ⊤ → ENNReal.ofReal a ≤ dimH A)) ∧ (∀ (k : ℕ) (A : Set (YuiEucl k)), 0 ≤ dimH A) ∧ (∀ (k : ℕ) (A B : Set (YuiEucl k)), A ⊆ B → dimH A ≤ dimH B) ∧ (∀ (k l : ℕ) (A : Set (YuiEucl k)) (g : A → YuiEucl l), Isometry g → dimH (Set.range g) = dimH A)", "explanation": "Complete N001. For positive a, ENNReal.ofReal a is the exact embedding, not a truncation; apply the native critical-exponent theorems with d : ℝ≥0 obtained from a and its positivity. Keep all four implications. Nonnegativity is in the extended nonnegative order. Prove cover monotonicity and the between-exponents contradiction of S004; for g on the subtype A, intersect covers with A and transport them in both directions using distance preservation, as in S005. Set.range g is precisely g(A); subtype distances are inherited ambient distances. Empty A is included.", "source_steps": [{"id": "S002", "text": "Under temporary premise L1: a<dimH(A), M003 gives muH(a)(A)=infinity, and F001 converts this to H^a(A)=infinity. Discharge L1, retaining that implication. Under temporary premise L2: a>dimH(A), M004 gives muH(a)(A)=0, and F001 converts this to H^a(A)=0. Discharge L2, retaining that implication. Eucl(k) has its Borel measurable structure by definition, and the arbitrary a>0 supplies the nonnegative-real source exponent in both applications. Universal introduction of a>0 yields the two clauses in F002."}, {"id": "S003", "text": "For a>0, if H^a(A)=0, the alternative dimH(A)>a contradicts the infinite-measure clause of F002. Thus dimH(A)≤a. If H^a(A)=infinity, the alternative dimH(A)<a contradicts the zero-measure clause, hence dimH(A)≥a. Nonnegativity follows from the [0,infinity]-valued supremum defining dimH. All these are implications with their displayed antecedents retained, not extra ambient assumptions. Since k,A and a>0 were arbitrary, universally quantify them to record F003, the full normalization and critical-exponent statements for every Euclidean set and positive exponent."}, {"id": "S004", "text": "Under the temporary premise L3: A⊆B, every delta-cover of B is a delta-cover of A, whence H^v_delta(A)≤H^v_delta(B) and H^v(A)≤H^v(B), for every v>0. If dimH(A)>dimH(B), choose a positive real exponent strictly between them; this is possible also if dimH(A)=infinity, since the smaller dimension must then be finite. The critical statements just established would give infinity=H^v(A)≤H^v(B)=0, a contradiction. This proves dimension monotonicity, discharging L3 with the antecedent A⊆B retained."}, {"id": "S005", "text": "Under L4: g:A→Eucl(l) preserves distances, intersect each ambient cover with A, map its members by g, and use these images to cover g(A). Conversely intersect a cover of g(A) with g(A) and take inverse images under g. Distance preservation makes g injective, and both constructions do not increase any member's diameter. Thus the two H^v_delta contents, and then the two H^v measures, are equal for every v>0 and delta>0. The same between-exponents contradiction as in S004 shows dimH(g(A))=dimH(A). This also treats empty A, whose contents vanish and dimension is 0. Discharge L4 and quantify k,A,l,g and retain each stated conditional premise to obtain every clause of the exported claim."}], "boundary": "Only listed inputs are project premises. The pinned Mathlib environment is freely available; no sibling proof is supplied.", "background_supplement_sha256": "1bf0b9c7347572e230e12960e1ad72681eb160b097abdd7fb42c849256a378a3", "objects": [], "representations": [], "bridges": [], "shared_inputs": [], "background_note_index": [{"id": "R001.BG002", "at": "/inference/argument/3/text", "kind": "mathematical", "fact": "For extended nonnegative costs, infima and suprema preserve pointwise order. In particular, if each cover in a target admissible class yields a source admissible cover of no greater cost, then the source infimum is at most the target infimum; the corresponding inequality persists after taking the supremum over positive scales. A pointwise inequality between nonnegative summands implies the same inequality between their series."}, {"id": "R001.BG003", "at": "/inference/argument/3/text", "kind": "elementary", "fact": "If 0≤p<q≤infinity in the extended nonnegative reals, there is a finite real v>0 with p<v<q. Consequently, two such extended values that have the same positive-exponent zero/infinity comparisons in F003 cannot be unequal."}]}}
